  "query_preview": "..\nFriday: Elevated to critical fire weather conditions are expected \nover the next 24 hours for portions of the Inland Northwest. Another \nround of showers and thunderstorms are developing on radar and \nsatellite with a deepening cu field in eastern Washinton and north \nIdaho already. There is ample instability leftover from today (1400 \nJ/kg on the 0Z KOTX sounding) to tap into still with steep lapse \nrates aloft. Synoptic ascent is maximed towards the Cascades with \nthe upper low just offshore the Washington border, but a weak vort \nmax will develop off the foothills of the Blue Mountains in the next \ncouple of hours supporting additional development further east in \neastern Washington. Red flag warnings continue for much of the \nregion outside of the Columbia Basin and Chelan county for abundant \nlightning until 7-11 AM this morning. Cannot rule out small hail up \nto penny size and gusty outflow winds up to 40 mph with these \nstorms. Storm coverage will decrease in the late morning as they \nmostly migrate into far southeastern BC. Expecting one more round of \nmore isolated showers and thunderstorms Friday afternoon (10-25% \nchance for the central Panhandle mountains) but the coverage of \nstorms is too low to warrant another lightning driven red flag \nwarning. Further west this afternoon, a dry cold front passage will \noccur and breezy southerly/southeasterly winds will pick up for the \nOkanogan Valley, Methow Valley, and Waterville Plateau, leading to \ncritical fire weather conditions. The Waterville Plateau Red Flag \nWarning will go a couple hours longer until 11 PM as the evening gap \nwinds bring a wind shift to the west. \n\nSaturday: Additional elevated to locally critical fire weather \nconditions are expected Saturday as the Inland northwest sits \nsquarely in the dry slot of the upper level low with a tightening \n500 mb height gradient. Winds will be west-southwest 12-15 mph with \ngusts around 15-20 mph with relative humidity around 14-22 percent. \nTemperatures will be cooler but still above average in the upper 80s \nto mid 90s with plentiful sun. \n\nSunday through Friday: Ensembles are in agreement of well above \nnormal temperatures continuing next week as the southwest regains \nstrength and retrogrades towards the 4 corners region by Monday. \nConditions will be fairly dry with highs in the 90s and 100s.\n/DB",
